Q: WPF InlineUIContainer displaying images incorrectly A: <p>I have come up with a work around that has solved this issue for me. I still do not fully understand why this is happening. The root cause seems to be buried in the framework. It may have something to do with <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/20561100/17264964">this answer</a>; <code>TextBlock</code> does not inherit from <code>Control</code> so without a <code>ControlTemplate</code> implicit styles don't get loaded as they normally would. Or potentially it may have to do with the <code>StretchDirection</code> property of <code>Image</code> being an alias for <code>Viewbox.StretchDirection</code> rather than a unique <code>DependencyProperty</code> of <code>Image</code>, although this seems unlikely. Regardless of the source of this behavior it is obvious there is an issue in the way the <code>Image</code> is measured before it is rendered stemming from the implicit <code>Style</code> being removed when the <code>Image</code> is removed from the visual tree.</p>
<p>Some things I tried: Hiding the <code>TextBlock</code> (<code>tb.Visibility = Visibility.Collapsed</code>) before clearing the <code>Inlines</code> collection and showing it again after adding to the collection will work for the example scenario but not the case where multiple sets of <code>Inline</code> objects are being swapped between. Forcing a redraw via <code>InvalidateVisual</code> after the <code>Image</code> has been rendered will fix the issue but it will cause an undesirable flickering effect.</p>
<p>The workaround: I have written a simple extension for <code>EventSetter</code> to attach a handler on load that will set the desired <code>StretchDirection</code> value on the <code>Image</code> control. This value will persist on the control when it has been removed from the visual tree so the problematic application of the implicit <code>Style</code> is not an issue.</p>
<pre><code>public class ImageSwapFixEventSetter : EventSetter
{
    public ImageSwapFixEventSetter() : 
        base(FrameworkElement.LoadedEvent, new RoutedEventHandler(Image_Loaded)) {}

    private static void Image_Loaded(object sender, RoutedEventArgs e)
    {
        if (sender is Image img)
        {
            img.Loaded -= Image_Loaded;
            img.StretchDirection = StretchDirection.DownOnly;
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>This can be used in an implicit <code>Style</code> and does not require direct manipulation of the <code>Image</code> controls from code behind.</p>
<pre><code>&lt;Style TargetType=&quot;Image&quot;&gt;
    &lt;local:ImageSwapFixEventSetter/&gt;
&lt;/Style&gt;
</code></pre>

Q: Using flags attribute in data access layer to generate bitwise SQL query A: <p>Your enum values are correct as powers of two, but usually it's better also add <code>None = 0</code> and change it to something like this:</p>
<pre><code>[Flags]
public enum ItemTags
{
    None      = 0,
    Browsable = 1 &lt;&lt; 1, // 2
    InMenu    = 1 &lt;&lt; 2  // 4
}
</code></pre>
<p>Also use or (<code>|</code>) not and (<code>&amp;</code>) to combine flags as follows:</p>
<pre><code>ItemTags.Browsable | ItemTags.InMenu // result = 6
</code></pre>
<p>The business layer usage mostly is correct and at the end you don't need to generate dynamic SQL for each flag. The best SQL Server query pattern is as follows:</p>
<pre><code>public List&lt;Item&gt; GetAllItems(ItemTags flags)
{
    using var conn = new SqlConnection(_connectionString);
    using var cmd = conn.CreateCommand();

    cmd.CommandText = @&quot; SELECT * FROM tableName WHERE (@flags = 0 OR (columnName &amp; @flags) = @flags)&quot;;

    cmd.Parameters.AddWithValue(&quot;@flags&quot;, (int)flags);

    conn.Open();

    using var reader = cmd.ExecuteReader();
}
</code></pre>
<p>To prevent of any error, make the column type as not nullable.</p>
